1
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:03,334
Come on!

2
00:01:06,437 --> 00:01:07,578
Let's go!

3
00:01:07,638 --> 00:01:23,951
Go, go, go, go, go, go, go!

4
00:01:24,011 --> 00:01:24,952
There we go.

5
00:01:33,824 --> 00:01:38,266
Slow down, slow down, slow down!

6
00:01:38,646 --> 00:01:41,808
Slow down, slow down, slow down!

7
00:01:42,008 --> 00:01:43,148
Okay, slow down.

8
00:02:02,234 --> 00:02:04,916
ah i want to find the edge

9
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:57,932
Mike, Mike, Mike, look at there.

10
00:02:58,552 --> 00:03:00,354
Mike, Mike, look at that.

11
00:03:00,594 --> 00:03:01,134
Look at that.

12
00:03:01,255 --> 00:03:02,696
You see that city out there, kid?

13
00:03:03,416 --> 00:03:04,117
See that big town?

14
00:03:04,197 --> 00:03:05,838
One day we're going to own that town, you and me.

15
00:03:05,858 --> 00:03:08,540
What do you think of that?

16
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:10,221
Yeah.

17
00:03:11,702 --> 00:03:12,703
This is my kid.

18
00:03:13,063 --> 00:03:18,868
There has to be a crossroad, a place to draw the line.

19
00:03:19,808 --> 00:03:25,175
And it comes down to the question, what is theirs and what is mine?

20
00:04:27,462 --> 00:04:31,164
Hey, Uncle Pat, you better be careful.

21
00:04:31,184 --> 00:04:32,484
You're going to throw that back out again.

22
00:04:32,504 --> 00:04:33,105
Don't worry about me.

23
00:04:33,125 --> 00:04:33,645
I can handle it.

24
00:04:33,665 --> 00:04:33,785
Yeah?

25
00:04:34,345 --> 00:04:38,107
So home from the wars, huh?

26
00:04:39,088 --> 00:04:39,588
How you doing?

27
00:04:40,028 --> 00:04:40,948
Oh, I'm doing fine.

28
00:04:41,329 --> 00:04:43,990
Servicing all the widows in the neighborhood now.

29
00:04:44,170 --> 00:04:45,110
Same old Uncle Pat.

30
00:04:47,111 --> 00:04:48,732
Flat beer from rusty pipes.

31
00:04:50,293 --> 00:04:51,714
If you only knew how I missed it.

32
00:04:52,714 --> 00:04:55,195
You might have changed a lot, but they shouldn't have beat the wise guy out of you.

33
00:04:55,615 --> 00:04:56,416
Not a chance of that.

34
00:04:57,209 --> 00:05:00,071
I saved the apartment for you, sold it to a couple of yuppies.

35
00:05:00,091 --> 00:05:01,332
They loved your mother's furniture.

36
00:05:01,712 --> 00:05:02,673
They said it was quaint.

37
00:05:04,474 --> 00:05:06,435
I still remember the day they came to repossess it.

38
00:05:07,436 --> 00:05:07,956
Ah, well.

39
00:05:08,716 --> 00:05:13,279
So what are you going to do now?

40
00:05:13,299 --> 00:05:13,840
Make a million.

41
00:05:15,401 --> 00:05:18,082
Well, in the meantime, I'll ask McDougal to give you a job while you're waiting.

42
00:05:18,323 --> 00:05:19,343
No way.

43
00:05:19,864 --> 00:05:21,004
It was good enough for your old man.

44
00:05:22,025 --> 00:05:22,986
So was arthritis.

45
00:05:23,586 --> 00:05:25,567
He supported a family, just like you're going to have to do.

46
00:05:26,188 --> 00:05:26,348
No.

47
00:05:27,328 --> 00:05:27,848
Not me.

48
00:05:28,229 --> 00:05:29,750
I am not falling into that trap.

49
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:32,472
Every man wakes up one fine morning with the wife and kids.

50
00:05:32,892 --> 00:05:33,633
Where'd they come from?

51
00:05:33,653 --> 00:05:34,934
They weren't there last time I looked.

52
00:05:34,954 --> 00:05:39,757
That's because most things in life, good and bad, just kind of happen to you.

53
00:05:40,998 --> 00:05:42,680
Well, I'm going to make things happen for me.

54
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:43,981
Sure you will.

55
00:05:45,121 --> 00:05:46,202
That'll be a buck for the beer.

56
00:05:49,264 --> 00:05:49,545
What?

57
00:05:50,919 --> 00:05:56,404
Your nephew comes home from serving his country, and he doesn't even rate a beer on the house?

58
00:05:56,664 --> 00:05:58,386
Well, you don't get rich giving things away.

59
00:05:58,626 --> 00:05:59,747
Oh, how do you get rich?

60
00:06:00,348 --> 00:06:02,610
I'm going to give you your first lesson on the house.

61
00:06:03,531 --> 00:06:06,554
I've been here 25 years, never bought a drink.

62
00:06:06,954 --> 00:06:07,294
Right, Eddie?

63
00:06:07,435 --> 00:06:07,635
Yeah.

64
00:06:08,485 --> 00:06:10,006
I could testify to that.

65
00:06:10,126 --> 00:06:13,027
In 1969, the Mets won the World Series.

66
00:06:13,727 --> 00:06:17,649
Eddie here starts piling on the bar, demanding, uh, a round on the house.

67
00:06:18,069 --> 00:06:19,470
Drinks for everybody, he says.

68
00:06:19,530 --> 00:06:20,610
Tell him what I did, Eddie.

69
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:22,991
He whacked me with a club.

70
00:06:23,971 --> 00:06:25,612
Almost knocked the eye out of my head.

71
00:06:26,432 --> 00:06:27,573
That's your way of making money?

72
00:06:28,213 --> 00:06:30,694
You outwork, outthink, outscheme, and outmaneuver.

73
00:06:31,274 --> 00:06:32,155
You make no friends.

74
00:06:32,395 --> 00:06:33,315
You trust nobody.

75
00:06:33,836 --> 00:06:37,377
And you make damn sure you're the smartest guy in the room whenever the subject of money comes up.

76
00:06:39,561 --> 00:06:40,682
I don't know, Uncle Pat.

77
00:06:41,642 --> 00:06:43,304
Doesn't sound like too much fun to me.

78
00:06:43,684 --> 00:06:43,964
Fun?

79
00:06:44,825 --> 00:06:46,226
You want fun, you go play at the beach.

80
00:06:46,766 --> 00:06:48,127
I think I'll try the city first.

81
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:49,167
Yeah, well, here.

82
00:06:50,348 --> 00:06:51,609
This'll get you there in style.

83
00:06:52,209 --> 00:06:52,530
Ooh.

84
00:06:53,490 --> 00:06:53,730
$10.

85
00:06:53,750 --> 00:06:54,351
Uncle Pat.

86
00:06:54,791 --> 00:06:58,734
Why don't you buy Eddie a binge on me, huh?

87
00:07:00,335 --> 00:07:02,036
Eddie, you gotta be quicker than that, man.

88
00:07:29,668 --> 00:07:31,269
I've always wanted to work on Wall Street.

89
00:07:31,610 --> 00:07:32,210
I read the journal.

90
00:07:32,230 --> 00:07:33,251
I know what's going on today.

91
00:07:33,971 --> 00:07:35,753
And I'm willing to do whatever it takes to succeed.

92
00:07:35,973 --> 00:07:39,516
I appreciate that, Flanagan, but all our professionals have college degrees.

93
00:07:39,536 --> 00:07:40,737
I catch on pretty quick.

94
00:07:40,877 --> 00:07:41,878
You're wasting your time.

95
00:07:41,898 --> 00:07:42,899
Just forget the street.

96
00:07:42,939 --> 00:07:43,039
No.

97
00:07:43,059 --> 00:07:43,679
We're not interested.

98
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:45,961
A guy like you, advertising.

99
00:07:46,221 --> 00:07:47,863
I have a feel for advertising.

100
00:07:47,943 --> 00:07:50,525
I mean, I can look at an ad and tell you what's wrong with it.

101
00:07:50,845 --> 00:07:53,567
Mr. Flanagan, we need solid credentials.

102
00:07:53,587 --> 00:07:55,869
No, a marketing degree is a prerequisite.

103
00:07:55,889 --> 00:07:56,490
Try the networks.

104
00:07:56,530 --> 00:07:58,652
In the Army, I spend a lot of time in communications.

105
00:07:58,672 --> 00:07:59,532
I think I can help you.

106
00:07:59,753 --> 00:08:01,934
Well, we need somebody who can hit the ground running.

107
00:08:01,954 --> 00:08:02,235
Sorry.

108
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:06,178
Maybe in six or eight months, but... You should pursue other avenues.

109
00:08:06,198 --> 00:08:06,738
Try something else.

110
00:08:06,758 --> 00:08:07,859
Maybe you could reenlist.

111
00:08:08,020 --> 00:08:09,541
I believe in positive thinking.

112
00:08:09,581 --> 00:08:10,181
Never quit.

113
00:08:10,422 --> 00:08:11,242
Never say die.

114
00:08:11,402 --> 00:08:12,603
I want to be part of the team.

115
00:08:12,804 --> 00:08:14,245
So, what do you think?

116
00:08:15,501 --> 00:08:17,123
Your resume's completely inadequate.

117
00:08:17,883 --> 00:08:19,104
I'm willing to start at the bottom.

118
00:08:19,605 --> 00:08:20,586
You're aiming too high.

119
00:08:20,806 --> 00:08:21,587
I'll do anything.

120
00:08:21,627 --> 00:08:23,028
You don't have enough experience.

121
00:08:23,068 --> 00:08:24,449
No, I need a job.

122
00:08:24,469 --> 00:08:26,011
We value education.

123
00:08:26,031 --> 00:08:27,452
We require a degree.

124
00:08:27,472 --> 00:08:28,533
You should go to college.

125
00:09:01,612 --> 00:09:02,412
Bar's closed, pal.

126
00:09:03,772 --> 00:09:05,032
I know, I'm looking for the manager.

127
00:09:05,773 --> 00:09:06,293
What's the matter?

128
00:09:07,713 --> 00:09:08,913
Find a hair in your quiche?

129
00:09:11,194 --> 00:09:12,954
No, I'm looking for a job.

130
00:09:13,294 --> 00:09:13,634
I see.

131
00:09:13,654 --> 00:09:15,835
You want to put a hair in somebody else's quiche.

132
00:09:17,328 --> 00:09:17,988
Just get canned?

133
00:09:18,748 --> 00:09:19,309
I'm looking.

134
00:09:19,709 --> 00:09:20,949
Looking for something better.

135
00:09:22,970 --> 00:09:23,830
Coughlin's law.

136
00:09:24,570 --> 00:09:26,491
Anything else is always something better.

137
00:09:28,011 --> 00:09:28,811
Coughlin's law?

138
00:09:29,691 --> 00:09:30,432
Douglas Coughlin.

139
00:09:31,152 --> 00:09:32,292
Logical negativist.

140
00:09:32,732 --> 00:09:34,653
Flourished in the last part of the 20th century.

141
00:09:35,353 --> 00:09:37,854
Propounded a set of laws that the world generally ignored.

142
00:09:38,594 --> 00:09:39,754
To its detriment.

143
00:09:40,715 --> 00:09:40,915
Me.

144
00:09:42,475 --> 00:09:43,355
Ever work behind the bar?

145
00:09:44,176 --> 00:09:45,436
My uncle's in the business.

146
00:09:47,382 --> 00:09:49,303
Do you know how to make a red-eye, Mr... what's your name?

147
00:09:49,523 --> 00:09:50,504
Brian Flanagan.

148
00:09:50,524 --> 00:09:52,225
No, I'm sorry.

149
00:09:52,285 --> 00:09:53,746
I haven't had the pleasure as yet.

150
00:09:53,986 --> 00:09:56,548
What about a vodka martini with a perno float?

151
00:09:57,589 --> 00:09:58,349
I'm a fast learner.

152
00:10:00,631 --> 00:10:02,832
Throw a 400-pound psychopath out of a bar?

153
00:10:04,213 --> 00:10:05,394
Guess I could if I had to.

154
00:10:06,174 --> 00:10:09,076
What about a 100-pound ballerina that's been speeding for three days?

155
00:10:09,556 --> 00:10:11,898
Just open the door and let her pirouette out in the street?

156
00:10:14,178 --> 00:10:16,819
These are just some of the tasks you'll be asked to perform, young Flanagan.

157
00:10:17,439 --> 00:10:19,980
This is the Upper East Side, the saloon capital of the world.

158
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:20,440
Big time.

159
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:23,381
Are you ready for the big time young Mr. Flanagan?

160
00:10:26,603 --> 00:10:27,543
I think I can handle it.

161
00:10:28,695 --> 00:10:31,317
Get your act together.

162
00:10:31,457 --> 00:10:32,478
Hey, come on, man.

163
00:10:32,598 --> 00:10:35,520
I've got four people waiting for me.

164
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:38,382
I'm not even going back there with three drinks.

165
00:10:38,582 --> 00:10:39,463
I know, it's coming.

166
00:10:39,483 --> 00:10:43,746
With a twist, an absolute on the rocks, skilfies and tonic, and a velvet hammer.

167
00:10:43,766 --> 00:10:46,548
This isn't what I ordered.

168
00:10:46,568 --> 00:10:47,768
What was that?

169
00:10:47,888 --> 00:10:49,930
A bosun and a cubaliva.

170
00:10:49,990 --> 00:10:50,991
Oh, I ordered.

171
00:10:51,631 --> 00:10:51,891
Woo!

172
00:10:52,091 --> 00:10:52,452
Ugh!

173
00:10:54,979 --> 00:10:57,381
If you're gonna sit in the water, go to Coney Island!

174
00:10:57,441 --> 00:11:00,062
You want me to bite the top off this?

175
00:11:00,162 --> 00:11:00,382
Yeah.

176
00:11:00,543 --> 00:11:01,903
This is a good recipe.

177
00:11:01,923 --> 00:11:03,524
Ordering a Cuba Libra.

178
00:11:03,604 --> 00:11:05,966
Could I please have a glass of water?

179
00:11:06,006 --> 00:11:07,667
She doesn't know how to make it.

180
00:11:07,707 --> 00:11:09,348
Where's my Jim Beam on the rock?

181
00:11:09,528 --> 00:11:10,048
It's coming.

182
00:11:10,209 --> 00:11:13,651
I'm not leaving till I get my Cuba Libra.

183
00:11:13,751 --> 00:11:14,591
Excuse me.

184
00:11:14,691 --> 00:11:16,092
Can I have an order, Nelson?

185
00:11:16,112 --> 00:11:17,113
This isn't what I ordered.

186
00:11:17,193 --> 00:11:19,014
Ordering a white card.

187
00:11:19,074 --> 00:11:20,015
You bitch!

188
00:11:20,935 --> 00:11:23,196
Why didn't you just tell me it was a rum and coke?

189
00:11:23,216 --> 00:11:25,436
Could I have what I ordered?

190
00:11:25,656 --> 00:11:26,396
An angel tip.

191
00:11:26,616 --> 00:11:27,556
A white wine.

192
00:11:27,656 --> 00:11:28,857
Pink squirrel.

193
00:11:28,937 --> 00:11:29,797
A fire top.

194
00:11:30,057 --> 00:11:30,757
A sing-a-ling.

195
00:11:30,917 --> 00:11:32,137
A white wine.

196
00:11:32,157 --> 00:11:33,438
Pink squirrel.

197
00:11:33,578 --> 00:11:34,178
Angel tip.

198
00:11:34,278 --> 00:11:35,258
Dirty mother.

199
00:11:35,278 --> 00:11:36,498
Could I have what I ordered?

200
00:11:36,658 --> 00:11:37,719
All right.

201
00:11:37,819 --> 00:11:38,879
All right.

202
00:11:38,919 --> 00:11:40,319
Now, what was it that you ordered?

203
00:11:40,419 --> 00:11:41,459
A martini.

204
00:11:42,259 --> 00:11:42,820
What's in that?

205
00:11:58,200 --> 00:11:59,981
Supposed to get 10% from the waitresses.

206
00:12:00,822 --> 00:12:01,603
I don't deserve it.

207
00:12:01,623 --> 00:12:02,443
You schmuck.

208
00:12:02,463 --> 00:12:03,844
You get money from girls every day?

209
00:12:03,864 --> 00:12:07,527
Listen, I'm sorry I called you a bitch.

210
00:12:08,107 --> 00:12:08,328
Why?

211
00:12:09,028 --> 00:12:09,589
I am a bitch.

212
00:12:12,651 --> 00:12:13,912
Got yourself a new disciple?

213
00:12:14,993 --> 00:12:16,033
Good night, my beautiful.

214
00:12:16,053 --> 00:12:18,175
Hey, frog man.

215
00:12:19,956 --> 00:12:22,418
Anyone that can get money out of her is a major talent.

216
00:12:23,279 --> 00:12:24,099
Come back next Thursday.

217
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:25,220
I've got five shifts open.

218
00:12:25,801 --> 00:12:26,842
You're offering me a job?

219
00:12:27,222 --> 00:12:27,442
Uh-huh.

220
00:12:29,905 --> 00:12:31,065
The waitresses hate me.

221
00:12:33,906 --> 00:12:35,427
Well, you wait till you've given them crabs.

222
00:12:36,688 --> 00:12:37,788
Then you'll really know hatred.

223
00:12:55,170 --> 00:13:00,412
Today, I'd like to continue our discussion of the demand for money by focusing on the money multiplier.

224
00:13:00,612 --> 00:13:04,654
We've got to add increases in currency, deposits, reserves, and bank credit along the whole system.

225
00:13:04,854 --> 00:13:06,674
Then what we have is the sum of an infinite series.

226
00:13:06,975 --> 00:13:12,777
Adding together the increases in currency and deposits, we find that they add up to 1 plus Cu over Cu plus Re.

227
00:13:13,137 --> 00:13:15,378
That simply stated is the money multiplier.

228
00:13:15,838 --> 00:13:16,799
Now, moving on.

229
00:13:17,699 --> 00:13:19,300
The essential technique of bartending.

230
00:13:22,690 --> 00:13:24,111
The less you pour, the more you score.

231
00:13:24,131 --> 00:13:26,672
The boss does better, we do better.

232
00:13:26,712 --> 00:13:27,353
And the customer?

233
00:13:27,913 --> 00:13:31,195
Think of the customer as a hurdler thinks of a hurdle, a means to an end.

234
00:13:31,735 --> 00:13:35,257
We dazzle him with ice work, we baffle him with bottle work.

235
00:13:36,038 --> 00:13:37,979
There are many ways to fool a customer.

236
00:13:38,559 --> 00:13:39,460
The short pour.

237
00:13:41,721 --> 00:13:42,922
The long pour.

238
00:13:44,082 --> 00:13:48,085
The ice mountain, the spring thaw, the speed rack shuffle, the hot shot.

239
00:13:48,605 --> 00:13:49,665
You will learn them all.

240
00:13:51,046 --> 00:13:52,107
Yes, Obi-Wan.

241
00:13:53,061 --> 00:13:55,283
Both hands, ladies, ladies, come on, come on.

242
00:13:55,363 --> 00:13:56,543
Okay, ready?

243
00:13:56,704 --> 00:13:58,004
One, two, three, let's go!

244
00:13:58,125 --> 00:14:02,207
Yeah, what does it mean?

245
00:14:03,088 --> 00:14:05,350
What does it mean?

246
00:14:05,990 --> 00:14:06,550
Nothing!

247
00:14:07,091 --> 00:14:08,171
Nothing?

248
00:14:08,792 --> 00:14:10,053
Hey, nothing?

249
00:14:10,913 --> 00:14:11,774
Whoa!

250
00:14:15,124 --> 00:14:18,126
Very sophisticated, man.

251
00:14:18,186 --> 00:14:19,147
Good job.

252
00:14:19,167 --> 00:14:21,028
You got talent.

253
00:14:21,048 --> 00:14:25,951
Come on, let's go with a drink.

254
00:14:26,031 --> 00:14:32,375
We're making money tonight.

255
00:14:32,415 --> 00:14:35,658
Let's make some money for a drink.

256
00:14:35,698 --> 00:14:36,959
Yeah.

257
00:14:37,159 --> 00:14:37,539
Ice.

258
00:14:41,520 --> 00:14:42,100
Thank you!

259
00:14:42,260 --> 00:14:47,123
I guess that's what you learn in college, right?

260
00:14:47,203 --> 00:14:48,704
Pretty fun, see?

261
00:14:48,804 --> 00:14:51,645
Don't forget my beer, Sprite!

262
00:14:52,046 --> 00:14:52,686
Missed me.

263
00:14:52,766 --> 00:14:53,126
Hang on!

264
00:15:10,339 --> 00:15:11,460
See, the name of the game is woman.

265
00:15:12,020 --> 00:15:17,663
Little darlings come in a-panting, their little hearts a-pitter-patting for the handsome, all-knowing bartender.

266
00:15:19,243 --> 00:15:24,626
And in their wake, a parade of slobbering geeks with one hand on their crutches and the other hand on their wallets.

267
00:15:26,027 --> 00:15:29,248
You get the women, you get the bucks, and boy, oh, boy, you've got them.

268
00:15:30,409 --> 00:15:32,330
Buttons were popping, skirts were rising.

